    "explainer": {
      "eyebrow": "Why both matter",
      "heading": "A total and a rate: *measurement needs both*",
      "intro": "The GHG Protocol gives organisations the total: how much they emit across a reporting period. SCI gives them the rate: how much carbon a piece of software produces for each unit of work it does. They answer different questions, and they are designed to be used together.",
      "items": [
        { "title": "The total", "tag": "GHG Protocol", "body": "One figure for the whole organisation, in tonnes of CO₂e and classified by scope. It is what regulators, investors and auditors need to judge an organisation's overall footprint. On its own it cannot show engineers which design decisions made it larger or smaller, and it moves with business growth." },
        { "title": "The rate", "tag": "SCI", "body": "Carbon per functional unit, such as per API request or per user, for a specific piece of software. It gives a team a target it can act on, and shows efficiency improving even while usage is growing." },
        { "title": "Together", "tag": "In practice", "body": "Software-level SCI scores can be aggregated into the totals that feed GHG Protocol inventories and reporting such as CSRD, which requires absolute figures. The rate guides the improvements; the total shows whether overall emissions are actually falling." }
      ],
      "closing": "GSF's view is that organisations need both. A total without a rate is hard to act on, and a rate without a total can hide growth.",
